exports[`Lumen & Loom Studio > Strategy System › 1. Situation & Direction > tile strategy-foundation-0 1`] = `
"Current SituationCurrent SituationiLumen & Loom Studio Ltd was incorporated in March 2025 by Maya Chen, Oliver Grant and Priya Shah and opened its Hackney Wick studio in April 2025. We sell a handwoven homeware collection online and at London markets, run beginner weaving workshops, and are starting bespoke commissions and a trade collection for interior designers and boutique hotels. Customers find us through Instagram, markets, word of mouth and design fairs. We are funded by £13,000 of founders’ capital, a £25,000 Start Up Loan and an £8,000 Hackney grant (half received at the opening). Monthly fixed costs are about £7,300, mostly three modest director salaries and rent; revenue starts small and is expected to cover costs from November 2025. To close a funding gap of about £14,200 before then, we are raising £20,000 from angels under SEIS (closing in August 2025) and £6,000 on Kickstarter in May 2025.RefreshiBack‹›Back
[fields] Lumen & Loom Studio Ltd was incorporated in March 2025 by Maya Chen, Oliver Grant and Priya Shah and opened its Hackney Wick studio in April 2025. We sell a handwoven homeware collection online and at London markets, run beginner weaving workshops, and are starting bespoke commissions and a trade collection for interior designers and boutique hotels. Customers find us through Instagram, markets, word of mouth and design fairs. We are funded by £13,000 of founders’ capital, a £25,000 Start Up Loan and an £8,000 Hackney grant (half received at the opening). Monthly fixed costs are about £7,300, mostly three modest director salaries and rent; revenue starts small and is expected to cover costs from November 2025. To close a funding gap of about £14,200 before then, we are raising £20,000 from angels under SEIS (closing in August 2025) and £6,000 on Kickstarter in May 2025."
`;

exports[`Lumen & Loom Studio > Strategy System › 1. Situation & Direction > tile strategy-foundation-1 1`] = `
"SWOT AnalysisSWOT AnalysisiYour VisioniBy 2030, to be London's best-known independent weaving studio: a self-sustaining team of eight makers, a collection stocked by twenty UK independent retailers, a waiting list for our workshops, and a verified low-carbon supply chain from sheep to sofa.Strengths1.Three complementary founders: craft, sales and operations2.Genuinely handmade in London with traceable British materials3.Two revenue types: products and workshops4.Lifetime repair service no competitor offersWeaknesses1.Limited production capacity (three looms)2.Cash runway of only a few months without new funding3.No trade track record yet4.Heavy reliance on Maya for weaving and teachingHow could these changes affect you?i12345Society & lifestyleThe economyRules & lawTechnologySupply & deliveryEnvironment & climateOpportunities1.Growing demand for sustainable, local interiors among designers and hotels2.Experience gifting and craft workshops booming in London3.Grant and SEIS-backed angel funding for creative businesses4.Retailer interest in UK-made homeware5.Changes in society & lifestyle could help you.6.Changes in environment & climate could help you.Threats1.Cost-of-living pressure on discretionary spending2.Rising wool and energy prices3.Larger brands launching “artisan” lines4.Rent review on the studio in 20275.Changes in the economy could hurt you.Back‹›Back
[fields] Three complementary founders: craft, sales and operations | Genuinely handmade in London with traceable British materials | Two revenue types: products and workshops | Lifetime repair service no competitor offers | Limited production capacity (three looms) | Cash runway of only a few months without new funding | No trade track record yet | Heavy reliance on Maya for weaving and teaching | Growing demand for sustainable, local interiors among designers and hotels | Experience gifting and craft workshops booming in London | Grant and SEIS-backed angel funding for creative businesses | Retailer interest in UK-made homeware | Changes in society & lifestyle could help you. | Changes in environment & climate could help you. | Cost-of-living pressure on discretionary spending | Rising wool and energy prices | Larger brands launching “artisan” lines | Rent review on the studio in 2027 | Changes in the economy could hurt you."
`;

